# Fluffer the Butler

An automated Slack bot built using the Hack Club tutorial framework and optimized to run seamlessly as a continuous background daemon service. Fluffer acts as an old, highly distinguished, and sarcastic British butler who delivers completely exaggerated, fake, and satirical tech news directly to your workspace channels.

---

## Key Features

* **The Old Butler Persona**: All news, alerts, and interactions are wrapped in custom butler formatting, phrases, and aristocratic greetings.
* **100% Reliable Tech Satire**: Features an internal database of highly exaggerated tech headlines to completely bypass strict external web scraping firewalls.
* **Multi-Interaction Model**: Responsive to direct Slack Slash commands (/fluffer-news) as well as direct @fluffer mentions.
* **Systemd Daemon Stability**: Fully optimized to automatically recover, keep running on system boot, and output telemetry logs cleanly via journalctl.

---

## Requirements and Environment Variables

The application requires Node.js (v18+) and standard Slack Bot Socket Mode configuration. To get started, configure a .env file in the root directory:

```env
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-your-slack-bot-token
SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=your-slack-signing-secret
SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-your-slack-app-token
```

---

## How to Deploy Globally on a Server

Follow these exact steps to link, load, and activate the background daemon on your host terminal:

### 1. Update Workspace Dependencies
Ensure the project packages are fully resolved:
```bash
npm install
```

### 2. Configure the Systemd Unit File
Create a global service mapping file at /etc/systemd/system/slackbot.service:

```ini
[Unit]
Description=Slack Bot
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=root
WorkingDirectory=/root/fluffer
ExecStart=/usr/bin/node index.js
Restart=always
Environment=NODE_ENV=production

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

### 3. Load and Launch the Process Instance
Execute these system commands to cache the profile configuration and fire up the engine:

```bash
# Reload systemd configuration cache files
systemctl daemon-reload

# Enable the bot to run continuously on host reboot and start it now
systemctl enable --now slackbot.service

# Restart the application sequence to load any active repository updates
systemctl restart slackbot.service
```

### 4. Monitor Application Telemetry
To read the live startup messages or debug incoming commands in real-time, stream the unit logs:
```bash
journalctl -u slackbot.service -f -n 50
```

---

## Usage Examples Inside Slack

Make sure to invite the bot to your target channel first using /invite @fluffer.

* **Slash Command**: Type /fluffer-news in chat to see your butler iron out the morning tech papers.
* **App Mention**: Ping @fluffer give me the latest news to fetch a random piece of technology gossip.
